# `core/actor`, driven the way a user drives it: a library in a repository, a
# suite beside it, and `buri test` — which on a toolchain with a native backend
# links a real binary and runs it.
#
# The conformance corpus holds `core/actor`'s semantics and holds them on
# *every* backend, and its build file says `platforms: [JS]` so that the
# reference run stays the reference one. What it cannot say is what a person
# gets: a package, a suite, one command. That is this case, and it is why the
# same claims appear twice.
#
# Every claim here is a value in an assertion rather than an exit code, and the
# last step is the negative twin the corpus rule asks for: one expectation
# flipped, and the report that comes back recorded whole. A green suite proves
# nothing about a failure mode nobody has seen fail.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "an actor answers, keeps its order, runs its mailbox down at the bound, stops once, and refuses everything after"

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/room", "--force"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "pass.txt"
}

# And again, from the cache: nothing about an actor is a fact about the machine.
run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/room"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "cached.txt"
}

# The negative twin. `.Err(.Stopped)` is the signature failure of the whole
# module — it is what a send, an ask and a second stop all answer once the
# mailbox is closed — so the report that records it is the one thing here that
# says the failure mode is real rather than asserted.
edit {
  file: "lib/room/test/counter.buri"
  replace: "assert.eq(counted.send(ctx, .Increment), .Err(.Stopped));"
  with: "assert.eq(counted.send(ctx, .Increment), .Ok(()));"
}

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/room", "--force"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "refused.txt"
}
